{"title":"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Inversion)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Inversion)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Isosceles triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c99bbe74cccdc92b249d376f13d9429d41a8b7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=AC$\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" />, is inscribed in circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be an arbitrary point inside <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/e/f8ed443e8c249a4e5eccbb4e0706591ce7c2bc05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD\\neq DC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" />. Ray <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> intersects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> again at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />). Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />) is chosen on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/2/0126ef22a96d12557a37abe02b291133cd593868.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DFE = 90^\\circ$\" width=\"105\" height=\"13\" />. Line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/b/52b3b006e6f9fe1681bd1f340795f789d888318e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FE$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> intersects rays <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> at points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/5/1e5f14d3ce4d3be22741a7232bcf139c968ec30e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle XDE = \\angle EDY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"143\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>All angle and side length names are defined as in the figures below. Figure 1 is the diagram of the problem while Figure 2 is the diagram of the Ratio Lemma. Do note that the point names defined in the Ratio Lemma are not necessarily the same defined points on Figure 1.\n</p><p>First, we claim the Ratio Lemma:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/8/fd8b8f24e059cd7deb80e41cc0d7a564291f6a54.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sin{\\theta_2}}{\\sin{\\theta_2’}}=\\frac{PB}{PC}\\cdot\\frac{b}{c}.\\]\" width=\"129\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>We prove this as follows:\n</p><p>By the Law of Sines of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/2/15228e3c3fa9e149613200891cfa8ef62dfd7fdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ABP},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/6/6562a868a03e93aed8ec800b8c690083249d37b9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sin\\theta_2}{BP}=\\frac{\\sin\\theta_1}{c}=\\frac{\\sin B}{AP}.\\]\" width=\"184\" height=\"37\" />\nBy the Law of Sines of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/9/819e14b1d048a25f3fb1c07636f2b10787f8f029.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ACP},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/4/51400526e75694a6110530ad44f700b1c4c3592b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sin\\theta_2’}{PC}=\\frac{\\sin\\theta_1’}{b}=\\frac{\\sin C}{AP}.\\]\" width=\"184\" height=\"38\" />\nDividing each of the corresponding equations, we get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/b/edb5f148a7915ffb1624f22c85bf06dff753775c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{PC}{PB}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\theta_2}{\\sin\\theta_1}=\\frac{b}{c}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin\\theta_1}{\\sin\\theta_1’}=\\frac{\\sin B}{\\sin C}.\\]\" width=\"255\" height=\"41\" />\nNoting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4d7527dde2a28dba35744007ba10ca130a43b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin \\theta_1= \\sin(180^\\circ - \\theta_1’)=\\sin \\theta_1’,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"248\" height=\"20\" /> we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/4/aa429c8f441e586c77e71ae269e4ef0527ca0ca8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sin\\theta_2}{\\sin\\theta_1}=\\frac{PB}{PC}\\cdot\\frac{b}{c}.\\]\" width=\"129\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>We transition into our problem.\n</p><p>By Power of a Point on circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/c/0dcdcd9c8cdbd7c4679fd83fca62a617ba3727fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/a/9fa54c74e9b4d2724651f2ced184912d55506089.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[XK\\cdot XD=XF\\cdot XE\\]\" width=\"177\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b27a4cbc4f8fa552d63c4ac5f5f929c85b282a3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[YL\\cdot YD=YF\\cdot YE.\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"12\" />\nBy power of a point on circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d267e485fb4b435196bf6c6671e60a09ce180fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/d/87d0157572921b1e087a28f693d6c104016e584c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[XB\\cdot XA=XF\\cdot XE\\]\" width=\"173\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c2485400a3888ff47be29486e86bb8fff9f5eb0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[YC\\cdot YA=YF\\cdot YE.\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"13\" />\nUsing the transitive property, we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/3/de3ecccc4653aa561f9818d02a34bdc1acfdb11a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[XK\\cdot XD=XB\\cdot XA\\]\" width=\"177\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448492dd0a8d7b9be544e4c9e9990de2e2f8822d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[YC\\cdot YA=YL\\cdot YD.\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"13\" />\nThus, by reverse Power of a Point, we have that quadrilaterals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc8a9e1bd1a900ae8c3c51e8c49f405922c0a809.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADKB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/3/7d3702b98525cf559475d9ad041755a5b142fa54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACLD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> are cyclic.\n</p><p>Now, we move to angle chasing.\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/b/e8b0e5923b6681e84c0a0d8721ff9aff3eb861a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ABD}=\\angle{AKD}=\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"178\" height=\"14\" /> (from cyclic <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc8a9e1bd1a900ae8c3c51e8c49f405922c0a809.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADKB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" />)</li></ul>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/4/8a4b11456558eb33e38e257c08dfbc07893cd7fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ABD}=\\alpha=\\angle{ACD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"175\" height=\"14\" /> (from the isosceles triangle)</li></ul>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/4381325ac465e2ceb077fbca5a445bc952b88ba5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ACD}=\\alpha=\\angle{ALD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"173\" height=\"14\" /> (from cyclic <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/3/7d3702b98525cf559475d9ad041755a5b142fa54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACLD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" />)</li></ul>\n<p>It is from here in which we focus on quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/8/0e85c0c86df7fe8aee1913eea3ed0760ba1e4791.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKEL.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/4/994eda45165945b404fa27fe9fff977e76a61293.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle KDE=\\beta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/9/179c8b67f027b2621d28555db0f716b9c2859112.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle EDL=\\gamma.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" /> Now realize that all we want to show, as given by the problem statement, is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12b36858b7fd3d131c741f0fde688eba5e8e1bd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\beta=\\gamma.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/a/fbaf94683693f20fb656457595f451cd62d6c1d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{KED}=90^\\circ-\\beta, \\angle{DEL}=90^\\circ-\\gamma.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"296\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We also have:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\angle LAD&amp;=180^\\circ-(\\angle ADL+\\alpha)\\\\\n&amp;=180^\\circ-((180^\\circ-\\gamma)+\\alpha))\\\\\n&amp;=\\gamma-\\alpha.\n\\end{align*}\nSimilarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/1/0219bd693a50e380c7d0d844cdef853d91eda157.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{KAD}=\\beta-\\alpha.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p>Calling back to what we want to show - if we want to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/5/60571a3cec5bbbc27033d7edcd62445ab260576f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\beta=\\gamma,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" /> it suffices to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/2/752f42c00ee8921853beb0f50bb00ae89bce3700.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle KAD=\\angle LAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"139\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae482f9e5aae5bdd3b3ba6420c0c4c67eeb8366.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle KED=\\angle LED.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"146\" height=\"14\" /> But this is equivalent to showing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/1/351c18f89d71f294b688cf75914854e8ce6fc7b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{AKE}\\sim\\triangle{ALE}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"149\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>However, applying our Ratio Lemma to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05ea476bf420dbbad5ba13ae8049f42fb6b54d93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{AKE},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/4/b843b4ea3e46ec94e586a9955634861216022895.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\sin 90^\\circ}=\\frac{AD}{DE}\\cdot\\frac{KE}{KA}.\\]\" width=\"163\" height=\"37\" />\nApplying our Ratio Lemma to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/8/8a88bdc1b96fa7b0a5733773ec62b388680e0765.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ALE},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/1/ff13089e7954431b369d1766176212e5fd5e4ac1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\sin 90^\\circ}=\\frac{AD}{DE}\\cdot\\frac{LE}{LA}.\\]\" width=\"159\" height=\"37\" />\nFrom the transitive property, we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b62496021af6df63be223cdd9af2452071eeb74.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{KE}{KA}=\\frac{LE}{LA}.\\]\" width=\"91\" height=\"37\" />\nNow, realize that we already have a shared side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> and equivalent angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/a/f4ae8916e74ef4cd88b868014b51bf963fa4e921.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ALE}=90^\\circ+\\alpha=\\angle{AKE}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"226\" height=\"16\" /> Thus, we establish a stronger bound between these two triangles - a congruence.\n</p><p>Thus, these two triangles are similar, and our proof is complete.\n</p><p><b>mathboy282</b>\n</p><p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2023USAJMOP6Diag.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/df/2023USAJMOP6Diag.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1073\" height=\"1197\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p><br />\n(cred. to v_Enhance diagram)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Inversion.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Inversion)\">Solution 2 (Inversion)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Inversion)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We realize that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/2/312384e111ded29c22cef9411d8a6298a6f3bdc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DFE = 90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"101\" height=\"14\" /> is quite annoying in calculations. Therefore, taking an inversion at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> will just make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/b/e8b7b98c0fed53adbc39bce64029ebb3dd7e33b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE\\perp EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" />, which is easier to incorporate. Also, many lines pass through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, we have intuition to invert at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> with some radius. \n</p><p>Take an inversion at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. We are not going to denote the inverted points with primes for simplicity. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/b/e8b7b98c0fed53adbc39bce64029ebb3dd7e33b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE\\perp EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" />. Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/e/36e7e585b1e8e1c819efe78bbb2b7ea830f0c3c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABD)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/c/28c601a202a8bea896cb57935f4fe42ce6db2ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(DFE)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" />, which means it lies on the radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/e/36e7e585b1e8e1c819efe78bbb2b7ea830f0c3c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABD)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/c/28c601a202a8bea896cb57935f4fe42ce6db2ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(DFE)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" />. Likewise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on the radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/0/6703cc61665bb89f2790f6d60122f0c25331adde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ADC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/c/28c601a202a8bea896cb57935f4fe42ce6db2ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(DFE)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" />. However, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0f6a692f19a65fe60fe5745826792f3fd1d57e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{EF}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> is the radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/5155aa866a797f98b826ed02392324eb8d7c0b5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/b/bebcab9015c214910dfde11c6604588f61af9e88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(DEF)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> is the radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/5155aa866a797f98b826ed02392324eb8d7c0b5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/b/83b6eec9dae3744181c79d5fead4415882980b89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ACD)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, so by concurrency of radical axis, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/e/d0ec1b91bb8762031b20a09b5e88c69f71534e65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC} \\cup \\overline{EF}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> lies on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/5/ba52c969bed0d101e945b960e9e2b96604fcadb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AX}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> (wow)! Meaning, we don't need to consider circle intersections, just line intersections. Now, we need to analyze what happens to the isosceles triangle.\n</p><p>Consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/7/0f7a55113a84ee4c04eb66f66d08310c32533ca2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACD$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> before inversion. Once you invert it, angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> goes to angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. Same with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/8/e08af8f3acc34996e3cba566dc970b7ed496ba4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ADB$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" />. However, angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is equal to angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> by isosceles! This means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is now the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/5862947b38a495ec9be80e5b9c7582de6cf0d269.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />.  A miracle!\n</p><p>Now we can phrase the question as follows:\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/b/cfbb4c52e011ceed6e813118a7d59e931af444a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AD}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> is the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1ac662f7ddbbce489953c8b7693848239564aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/b/cfbb4c52e011ceed6e813118a7d59e931af444a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AD}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> intersect the circumcircle at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and draw a line perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/8/5e8bc36793a7c64d05fff20d519f0f72d9256fab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AE}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/2/9b25f8e64b484493fda944d25cad453423041fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l$\" width=\"5\" height=\"12\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/2/1e270e32644b9b8e6271eb7251602482e0f2d96f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}\\cup l=X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"96\" height=\"18\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/2/932d2a4969572acc8ec8065f35e819858d6cebcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB} \\cup l=Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />. Prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/7/27765a6342184d615dfebd93ec7961a152d35d39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle YDE = \\angle XDE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"143\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><br />\nOne glance and one realizes the question is extremely easy. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/8/5e8bc36793a7c64d05fff20d519f0f72d9256fab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AE}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> is a angle bisector and altitude of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/8/588f748c9951c8d48fe883cf0320704e0a6e52a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AXY$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, meaning that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/3/5e3fb33066cd9149f54ba2cc311110eea577eafe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{YE}=\\overline{EX}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/9/699784c989c3db382520e6f92819e062d239f504.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{DE}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> is an altitude of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/0/df0f7e67c42d3bd8e50c1fdb803d0e9ca4067c4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DYX$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/3/5e3fb33066cd9149f54ba2cc311110eea577eafe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{YE}=\\overline{EX}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" />, so of course <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/9/699784c989c3db382520e6f92819e062d239f504.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{DE}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> is the angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/b/d8b4b0a5557cae77a0958f9a3a68749dd447d781.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle YDX$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" />! The problem is proven!\n</p><p>~MATHLOVERSSD\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/FsduivxtoWE\">https://youtu.be/FsduivxtoWE</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO\" title=\"2023 USAJMO\">2023 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2023\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Question</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2023 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017050452\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.058 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.105 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 597/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1295/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 55/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19833/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   29.728      1 -total\n 79.61%   23.665      1 Template:USAJMO_newbox\n 19.68%    5.851      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:25862:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017050452 and revision id 274912. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}